# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (semantics & runtime)
# Var lifetime + scope, struct locals, init order, fn main, static `var buf[N]`
# inside library fns, the allocator's process-lifetime persistence + arena
# exhaustion policy, i64::MIN arithmetic, and thread handle lifetime.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10: the global bump allocator has CHAINED
# CHUNKS since v6.1.19 (measured — 400 MB in 100 MB bites succeeds), so the
# old "exhaust the heap segment in 9 allocs" story no longer describes it;
# three entries added from v6.5.8/.9.
# 33 entries (DERIVED at 6.6.16: grep -c "^\[\[entries\]\]"; this line read 27 while the file held
# 30, and earlier 17 while it held 18).
# v6.6.16: +3 entries — closure_returns_belong_to_the_closure_since_6_6_16, pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16,
# typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616 (its `p + n` step table SUPERSEDED at 6.6.17 by sizeof(T) everywhere —
# language/features.cyml typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617). Updated: the `_str` suffix entry (what "Str-typed" means — globals,
# fields, call results; the dispatcher's capture rung), global_init_order_silent_zero (enum-naming
# and all-constant struct / array initializers are baked; the x86 kernel late-replay warning).
# v6.6.5: `private` covers the whole file, and forward references are checked; a struct
# declared inside a fn is per call and per thread, and an over-budget array local warns that
# its storage is static.

[[entries]]
name = "heap_allocation_patterns"
description = "How to structure heap-allocated data in a language without structs-on-heap"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has structs, but for heap data you typically use raw alloc + store64/load64.
The pattern that emerged across both projects:

1. POINTER + ACCESSOR FUNCTIONS:

   var map_nodes = 0;
   fn map_alloc() { map_nodes = alloc(1024 * 112); }
   fn map_node_x(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112); }
   fn map_node_y(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112 + 8); }

   This is essentially manual vtable-free OOP. The accessor functions ARE
   your type safety. Name them consistently: module_field(index).

2. ENUM FOR LAYOUT CONSTANTS:

   enum BspNode {
       BSP_NODE_SIZE = 112;
       BSP_NODE_X = 0;
       BSP_NODE_Y = 8;
       BSP_NODE_DX = 16;
   }

   Enums cost zero gvar_toks. Use them for every struct layout.

3. LAZY INIT GUARD:

   fn framebuf_set_palette(data) {
       if (palette == 0) { palette = alloc(768); }
       memcpy(palette, data, 768);
   }

   We found a real segfault from this: framebuf_set_palette() was called
   before framebuf_init() allocated the buffer. The lazy guard fixed it.
   Always guard heap pointers that might be called before init.

4. PACK SMALL STATE:

   # Instead of 3 globals:
   var player_start = 0;
   fn map_find_player_start() {
       if (player_start == 0) { player_start = alloc(24); }
       store64(player_start, x);
       store64(player_start + 8, y);
       store64(player_start + 16, angle);
   }
   fn player_start_x() { return load64(player_start); }

[[entries]]
name = "enum_as_namespace"
description = "Using enums as zero-cost namespaced constants"
category = "cyrius"
---
Enums in Cyrius are compile-time integer constants. They don't consume
gvar_toks slots — they const-fold, and only NON-literal top-level
`var X = expr;` inits take a gvar_toks slot at all. (This line used to
say "a separate initialization path in cc2"; `cc2` has not been the
compiler since the v3.x era — the current one is `cycc`. See
`diagnostics_caps.cyml::the_gvar_wall` for the live table.)

This makes them perfect for constants that would otherwise eat your
global variable budget:

  # BAD: 8 gvar_toks slots
  var MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
  ...

  # GOOD: 0 gvar_toks slots
  enum MapMax {
      MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
      ...
  }

Both produce identical code — the values are inlined at use sites.

WARNING: Enum VALUES must be non-negative integers — `enum E { NEG = -1; }`
still errors 'expected number, got -' (verified v6.4.2). For a negative
constant, declare it as a top-level `var FIXED_NEG_ONE = 0 - 65536;` or
`var FIXED_NEG_ONE = -65536;` — negative literals DO work in general
expressions now; it is only the enum-value grammar position that rejects
the leading `-`.

In cyrius-doom, converting constants to enums saved 30+ gvar_toks slots,
which made the difference between compiling and not compiling.

[[entries]]
name = "var_name_bracket_is_bytes_not_elements"
description = "`var name[N]` in a FUNCTION is N bytes (rounded up to 8); the SAME `var name[N]` at TOP LEVEL is N i64 SLOTS = N*8 bytes. Two different sizes for one syntax. Use `var a: i64[N]` (v6.2.1) to mean slots in both positions."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TWO MEANINGS — this is the part that bites hardest, and it is
NOT what the original note below said:

    fn f() {
        var buf[16];       # 16 BYTES (rounded up to a multiple of 8)
    }
    var buf[16];           # TOP LEVEL: 16 i64 SLOTS = 128 BYTES

Same three tokens, an 8× difference, decided by whether you are inside
a function. The top-level form was fixed to N*8 at v6.4.10 (it had been
inconsistent); the fn-local form has always been bytes.

WHERE THE TOP-LEVEL FORM BIT US: **CVE-34** (fixed v6.4.81). A
compiler-internal `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is 2048 bytes, not 256 — and
the code that copied into it had been reasoned about as if it were 256.
Reading a bare `var x[N]` and mentally assigning it N bytes at top
level is a live source of both over- and under-estimates.

THE FIX — SAY WHICH YOU MEAN (v6.2.1):

    var a: i64[N];     # N i64 slots = N*8 bytes — SAME in fn and top level
    var a: u8[N];      # N bytes explicitly — SAME in both
                       # widths i8..i64 / u8..u64, u128

The element width is registered into the var-size table, so every
backend (x86 / aarch64 / macho / pe / cx) inherits it. Prefer the
annotated form in new code; it removes the position dependence entirely.

THE ORIGINAL BITE (fn-local, still accurate):

    var buf[16];       # 16 bytes — OK for a 2-u64 pair
    var state[25];     # 25 BYTES — NOT 25 u64 lanes!
    var lanes[200];    # 200 bytes = 25 × u64 lanes

Surfaced v5.4.15 writing `lib/keccak.cyr` — `var state[25]` gave
25 bytes (should have been 200). First `memset(state, 0, 200)`
overwrote 175 bytes of adjacent stack locals including the
caller's return-address region. SIGSEGV on the first absorb.

RULE OF THUMB for the unannotated form: inside a fn, size it as
`N * 8` (or `N * sizeof(thing)`) yourself; at top level, do NOT — the
compiler already multiplied.

[[entries]]
name = "stack_var_keyword_for_struct_locals"
description = "v5.5.36 `stack var p: Point;` forces a named struct onto the frame; `var p: Point;` also now parses"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added the `stack` storage-class keyword for named struct
locals, plus `var p: Point;` (no RHS) as a parse-level way to
declare a struct-typed local. Both allocate on the frame:

    fn demo() {
        stack var p: Point;       // explicit (preferred when
                                  // you want readers to notice)
        p.x = 1;
        p.y = 2;
    }

    fn demo2() {
        var q: Point;             // same effect, shorter form
        q.x = 3;
    }

`stack` is reserved (TOK=111). Variable and field names can't
use `stack` any more — rename to `stk`/`sp` if you collide.

Struct locals that previously needed anonymous filler `var`
slots to land at a known rbp disp can now just declare at the
struct type directly.

[[entries]]
name = "fn_main_is_not_the_entry_point"
description = "⛔ CORRECTED v6.6.5, RE-MEASURED v6.6.14 — CYCC AUTO-CALLS `main()` (since v5.9.37), and this entry said the opposite for eighteen minors. The live trap is the reverse: a source that defines `fn main` AND calls it at top level without exiting runs the body TWICE and exits with the SECOND run's value — a trailing `var r = assert_summary();` is dead for the exit code. Top-level statements still run first; `main` is called after them."
category = "cyrius"
---
⛔ **CORRECTED v6.6.5, RE-MEASURED v6.6.14.** At the end of the entry code `src/main.cyr` looks
`main` up in the fn table (`_find_fn_by_name(S, "main", 4)`) and emits `call main` when it has a
body; the program then exits with main's return value. This entry said the opposite for eighteen
minors, and `language/agents.cyml` kept saying it until 6.6.14.

Measured on cycc 6.6.14 (`./build/cycc < p.cyr > p && ./p`):

| source | stdout | exit |
|---|---|---|
| `fn main(): i64 { return 42; }` | — | 42 |
| `fn main() { syscall(60, 42); return 0; }` | — | 42 |
| no `fn main`; top level ends `var r = helper();` (helper returns 5) | — | 5 |
| `fn main` (prints, returns `count + 10`), then `var ec = main(); var r = 7;` | body TWICE | **12** — the second run; `r` ignored |
| the same, then `syscall(60, ec);` after the call | body once | 11 |
| `fn main` with a failing `assert_eq`, then `var ec = main(); var r = assert_summary();` | FAIL twice, `0 passed, 1 failed` | **0** |

⭐ **THE LIVE TRAP fails SILENTLY GREEN.** The auto-call is unconditional — it does not know you
already called `main` — so the last row reports a failure and exits 0. At 6.6.5 a control build
of `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_shm_fd_passing.tcyr` printed `19 passed, 30 failed` and exited 0;
at 6.6.14 a draft TLS test in the same shape hid 42 failed assertions.

THREE SHAPES ARE CORRECT:
  (a) no `fn main` at all — top level ends on the value to exit with (`var r = assert_summary();`
      then `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);`);
  (b) `fn main` that RETURNS the value and is NOT called at top level — the auto-call runs it once;
  (c) `fn main` called at top level FOLLOWED BY an explicit `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);`, which exits
      before the auto-call is reached. This is what the `tests/tcyr/crossos/` syscall tests use.

THE MECHANISM (from the v5.6.33 entry, which predates the auto-call): top-level statements ARE
the program. The entry branches over every fn body, runs enum inits, global inits and the
top-level statements in source order, then — since v5.9.37 — `call main` if one exists, and exits
with main's return, else with the last top-level `var`'s value. Before v5.9.37 a source holding
only `fn main() { … }` exited with whatever was in rax / x0 (argc on Mach-O arm64, so 1), which
is how a "macOS arm64 runtime regression" was filed at v5.6.33 against a fixture whose body never
ran. Two wrong conclusions, both from reading the doc instead of running the compiler.

[[entries]]
name = "var_buf_in_library_functions"
description = "`var buf[N]` inside a library function is static data — every call shares the SAME buffer, and any Str borrowing from it dangles after the next call"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf[262144];                       # NOT stack — static data
        var n = file_read_all(path, &buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(&buf, n));        # Str's data ptr → static buf
    }

This is the compounded form of "var buf[N] is BYTES not ELEMENTS."
NOTE (v6.3.15 default-on stack arrays): a fn-local `var buf[N]` now
behaves TWO ways by size — SMALL arrays route to per-thread STACK
slots (so a returned `&buf` DANGLES after the fn returns, reused by
the next call), while OVERSIZED arrays (over the per-fn stack budget,
as with `var buf[262144]`) STILL fall back to one shared global
buffer (the compiler emits `note: oversized array local kept in
shared global ... use alloc()` plus the `large static data` warning).
Either way the borrow is unsafe across calls. Sized properly, the
first call works; the second call clobbers the first — every Str the
first call returned now points at the second file's bytes (shared
global) or at reused stack (small).

You see this as parsed values bleeding across calls — IDs become
suffixes/prefixes of later IDs, descriptions become fragments of
the most-recently-read file. Nothing crashes; everything is just
wrong.

The build emits a hint:

    warning: large static data (262144 bytes) — consider alloc()
    for buffers >4K

Heed it. Library helpers that hand out heap pointers must heap-
allocate the backing memory:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf = alloc(262144);               # fresh per call
        var n = file_read_all(path, buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(buf, n));
    }

Now the Str's data ptr aliases an alloc that lives until process
exit; consecutive calls keep their backing memory.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

vidya 2.3.0 — `lib/toml.cyr::toml_parse_file` shipped with
`var buf[262144]`. `vidya stats` segfaulted on the first
content-loading command after the cyrius 4.5.0 → 5.7.0 upgrade
because every concept's parsed Str pointed into the *last*
concept.toml's bytes; `concept_id` returned 0/garbage and
`path_join(0, fname)` faulted at NULL+offset. Workaround: read
the file into `alloc(N)` in the caller and call `toml_parse(...)`
directly, bypassing the leaky helper. Permanent fix belongs
upstream in cyrius stdlib.

This trap is invisible at single-call test scope. Every stdlib
helper that returns Str/buf-borrowing values must be exercised
with at least two consecutive calls in tests, otherwise the bug
sleeps until the second consumer arrives.

[[entries]]
name = "global_init_order_silent_zero"
description = "✅ FIXED in v5.7.32 (cyrlint warning) + v5.7.36 (string-literal awareness) — original bug behavior + workaround pattern preserved below for pre-v5.7.32 toolchain users"
category = "cyrius"
---
⭐ **6.6.16 — WHAT IS A CONSTANT INITIALIZER NOW.** An initializer that names an enum
constant (`var x = A;`, `E.A`, `A + 1`, `var x: u8 = A;`), the enum declared above or below,
is a constant: baked into the image like `var x = 42;`. The name resolves as a read of it
does — a later global that hides the enum constant wins. So is a struct literal whose every
field is a constant, and an array list (`var t: i64[3] = {1, 2, E.A};`). The startup store
still runs; the visible difference is an EARLIER initializer reading it, which now sees the
value instead of 0. KERNEL BUILDS: an x86 `kernel;` build runs every OTHER initializer after
the top-level program (the multiboot shim must run first), so a kernel that never returns
never runs them; the compiler warns once per such declaration (not for a host build, an
aarch64 kernel, or an EFI application with `efi_main`). Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/
gotchas.cyml `a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write`.

⚠ **RE-MEASURED at v6.6.5 — the example below no longer reads 0, and the rule now sees
wrapped initializers.** Since v6.4.74 a global whose initializer is a CONSTANT is static-
folded, so `var COMPUTED = FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C;` over later `var FLAG_A = 0x1;` …
evaluates to 7 (measured). The silent zero is real for a later global with a COMPUTED
initializer: `var C = FLAG_A | 2;` over a later `var FLAG_A = f(1);` exits 2, not 3.
cyrlint warns on both shapes. Before v6.6.5 it scanned only the declaration's own line, so
a WRAPPED initializer — `var A = 1 +` / `    B;` over a later `var B = g();` — drew no
warning while the binary read B as 0 (exit 1, the reordered program exits 3); it now scans
to the statement's `;` across lines, ignores an `=` inside a trailing comment, and checks a
second `var` after a `;` on the same line. A `}` inside a multi-line string no longer takes
a real top-level declaration out of its sight. CHANGELOG [6.6.5].

✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.7.32. `cyrlint`'s `lint_globals_init_order`
walks the file twice (pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT =
...;` with line, pass 2 walks every `var X = expr;` initializer
and emits a warning if any IDENT in the expr was declared at a
line > X's line). v5.7.36 added string-literal awareness so
identifier-shaped substrings inside `"..."` literals don't
false-positive. Tracked at:
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/archived/2026-04-28-global-init-order-forward-ref.md`
(v5.7.32 fix; archived 2026-05-03 during the v5.8.41 cyrlint
cleanup pass). Body below preserved as the v5.8.47 vidya audit
cross-referenced this entry as the canonical example of the
"description was stale; body had the correct upstream status"
pattern.

PRE-v5.7.32 BUG (kept for historical context):

Cyrius initializes top-level `var X = expr;` declarations in source
declaration order. If `expr` references a constant declared LATER in
the same file, that reference resolves to `0` (the default
zero-initialized value) at the time `X` is evaluated. No warning,
no error — `X` ends up holding the wrong value, and downstream
consumers silently see the wrong value too.

    # In src/foo.cyr (single file, declaration order matters):

    var COMPUTED = FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C;   # → 0, NOT 7

    # ... 200 lines later ...

    var FLAG_A = 0x1;
    var FLAG_B = 0x2;
    var FLAG_C = 0x4;

    # COMPUTED reads as 0 throughout the program.

A trivial assertion surfaces it instantly:

    fn test() {
        assert_eq(COMPUTED, FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C, "computed = OR");
        # → fails with "got 0, expected 7"
    }

WHERE THIS BIT US:

mabda v3 Step 4f.ii (BO page-perms tightening). Added named
perm-bitmask constants near the top of `src/backend_native.cyr`:

    var _NATIVE_PERM_FULL = AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_READABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_WRITEABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_EXECUTABLE;

at line 117. The `AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` constants lived at line 391+ in
the same file. Result: `_NATIVE_PERM_FULL` evaluated to `0` at load
time. Every BO got mapped with `perms = 0` — no read, no write, no
execute. Every dispatch TDR'd at the 10-second AMDGPU timeout with
"post-dispatch marker stale" + "output unchanged."

Visible failure mode looked like a wedged GPU — ~30 minutes spent
on hardware-iteration hypotheses (firmware near-permanent-wedge
rule, ring corruption, bad VAs) before a CPU regression test
`assert_eq(_NATIVE_PERM_FULL, R|W|X)` returned `got 0, expected 14`
and pinned the actual cause.

Fix: one-line move. `_NATIVE_PERM_*` block relocated below the
`AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` defs. Code that was "wrong since the moment I
typed it" started working immediately on hardware.

WORKAROUND PATTERN until the lint lands:

  1. For computed-from-other-constants globals, place the
     definition AFTER its dependencies (forced reordering).
  2. Add CPU regression tests that assert the *value* of every
     computed perm/flag/size constant. These are 5-line tests
     that catch this class of bug before any hardware
     iteration.
  3. When refactoring top-level declarations, re-run the value
     assertions. Reordering is a top-level change with non-local
     consequences in Cyrius.

UPSTREAM STATUS:

**✅ Shipped at v5.7.32 + v5.7.36** — picked option (a):
`cyrlint` rule `lint_globals_init_order` walks the file
twice. Pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT = ...;`
and records `(name, line)`. Pass 2 walks every initializer
expression, scans IDENT tokens, and emits a warning if any
IDENT was declared at a line LATER than the current decl:

    warn line N: global var init refs 'NAME' declared at line M
                 (silent zero at init)

v5.7.36 extended the Pass-2 scanner with **string-literal
awareness** — IDENTs nested inside `"..."` strings or `'...'`
chars (with `\\` / `\"` / `\'` escapes) no longer trigger
spurious warnings. So `var MSG = "FLAG_LATER not yet
defined"; var FLAG_LATER = 1;` is clean.

The compile-time error path (option b) was rejected because
existing stdlib shapes rely on declaration order in benign
ways; promoting to error would break green builds across
the ecosystem. Lint warning is the right level — surfaces
the foot-gun without forcing churn.

Affects ANY computed-from-other-constants global: size
calculations, page perms, bit-packed enum compositions,
refactors that reorder top-level declarations, the common
"define module constants near the top of the file for grep-
ability" pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "bump_allocator_exhaustion_in_tests"
description = "The stdlib bump allocator never frees during process lifetime — but since v6.1.19 it CHAINS fresh 256 MB chunks instead of failing, so the DOOM/mabda-era 'exhausted at the 9th alloc(112)' symptom is retired. alloc() returns 0 only for size<=0, size>ALLOC_MAX (2 GiB), or a failed mmap. The no-free property is still real; use an arena when you need to reclaim."
category = "cyrius"
---
⚠ **CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10), by running it.** The MECHANISM
below is out of date and the numbers with it. `lib/alloc.cyr` has
been a CHUNKED bump allocator since **v6.1.19**: it bumps inside the
current chunk and, on overflow, `mmap`s a FRESH 256 MB chunk
(`_LINUX_CHUNK = 0x10000000`) and continues there. Old chunks stay
mapped and valid; a single allocation never spans two. Measured on
this box: 4 × `alloc(100000000)` = 400 MB, crossing the first chunk,
all non-zero.

So today `alloc(N)` returns 0 in exactly three cases:

    N <= 0                       # rejected
    N  > ALLOC_MAX (0x80000000)  # 2 GiB single-allocation cap
    the mmap for a new chunk failed

"Ran out of heap after nine 112-byte contexts" is no longer reachable.
If you are chasing a null from `alloc` today, check the SIZE first —
a negative or overflowed size argument is the live cause, not
cumulative pressure.

⛔ **What IS still true, and is the reason to keep reading:** nothing
is ever freed. `alloc` has no `free`. A long-running process that
allocates per request grows without bound, and the only batch release
is `alloc_reset()`, which **invalidates EVERY pointer the allocator
has ever handed out** — including ones the stdlib itself is holding.
Any `Str`, `Vec`, `HashMap`, arena or struct built before the reset is
dangling afterwards, and the span is zeroed AND re-issuable, so a
stale pointer reads zeros until something else is allocated over it
and then reads that. v6.5.7 fixed one instance of this *inside the
stdlib* — `default_alloc()` memoized its vtable in the very arena it
describes, so `alloc_reset()` invalidated the allocator itself and
every later `default_alloc()` returned a dangling pointer
(reproducible SIGSEGV, hisab-filed). The vtable now lives in a
dedicated `_default_allocator_storage` global outside any resettable
region. **Consumer-held pointers are still the caller's problem.**
Reset only when nothing from the previous epoch will be read again.

For per-request memory you actually want reclaimed, use an ARENA
(`arena_new` / `arena_new_growable` / `arena_reset`) rather than
leaning on the global bump — and read
`arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid` in this
file before you size one.

ORIGINAL (DOOM/mabda-era, kept for historical context — the WRITE
IDIOM at the bottom is still good advice, the diagnosis is not):

The Cyrius stdlib `alloc` is a bump allocator: each `alloc(N)`
moves a global pointer forward by N bytes; allocations are
NEVER freed during process lifetime. This is fine for short-
running programs (test suites, benchmarks, smoke programs) but
breaks down when a single process accumulates enough
allocations to exhaust the underlying heap segment.

**Symptom.** `alloc(N)` silently returns 0. Subsequent `store*`
to the returned pointer dereferences null → segfault. The
crash looks like an unrelated bug in test code that seemed to
work before.

**Where it bites.** Test files that grow over time and run all
their tests in one `main()` invocation. Each test that calls
`alloc(N)` permanently consumes that N. After enough tests,
the cumulative consumption exceeds the heap segment.

**Diagnostic.** If a test fails with exit 139 (SIGSEGV) and
inserting `assert(ctx != 0, "alloc returned non-null")`
catches it, the bump allocator is exhausted. mabda hit this
during Step 7.7 — adding the 9th `alloc(112)` ctx in
`tests/tcyr/mabda_v3_phase_d.tcyr` made `alloc` start
returning 0.

**The fix.** Stack-local arrays for test-scoped buffers:

```cyrius
# Before — heap-allocated, accumulates:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx = alloc(112);
    memset(ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}

# After — stack-allocated, freed at fn return:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx[112];
    memset(&ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}
```

The `var name[N]` form allocates on the stack, gets reclaimed
at fn exit. Identical observable behavior for test code; zero
heap pressure.

**When you MUST use alloc.** When a value needs to outlive the
fn (heap-allocated structs returned from constructors,
caller-owned buffers passed across abstraction boundaries).
Production code generally needs alloc; test code generally
doesn't.

**Why not fix the bump allocator.** The bump-allocator design
is intentional — its simplicity is the load-bearing property
for AGNOS's "no GC, no malloc" stance. Adding a heap-replacing
allocator would change the contract for every consumer.
Better to teach the test-write idiom: stack-local for
test-scoped buffers.

**Cross-pollination.** Every AGNOS test suite hits this
eventually. Teach the pattern at the start; don't wait for
the segfault. samvada's `tests/samvada.tcyr` already uses
stack-local idiom; mabda's older test files retrofitted as
they grew.

[[entries]]
name = "unwrap_is_option_only_result_silently_corrupts"
description = "unwrap()/unwrap_or() operate on Option (Some/None); on a Result they silently return the raw error code instead of aborting — use result_unwrap/is_ok for Results. Still true under the v6.6.0 VALUE FORM; only the spelling changed (payload() is DELETED, unwrap takes (t, v))"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Updated at v6.6.1 for the value form. The HAZARD IS UNCHANGED — only the
spelling is.** That is the point worth carrying: the flip re-aritied every helper
in this entry and did not close the hole, because the hole is the overlapping tag
values, not the representation.

`unwrap(t, v)` (lib/tagged.cyr) checks ONLY `is_none()` (tag == None == 0) before
returning `v`. A `Result` (lib/result.cyr) has Ok tag=0, Err tag=1 — tag values that
OVERLAP Option's None=0/Some=1. So calling `unwrap()` on a Result is a category
error with NO diagnostic:

- an **Err** (tag=1, != None) sails past the `is_none` check → `unwrap` hands back
  the payload = the RAW ERROR CODE as if it were a valid value (e.g. a negative
  fd fed straight into a syscall);
- an **Ok** (tag=0 == None) makes `unwrap` ABORT with "unwrap called on None".

Because the tags overlap, the mismatch never faults at the unwrap site — it
CORRUPTS DOWNSTREAM. This bit the async net client: `lib/net.cyr` fns
(`tcp_socket`, `sock_accept`, `sock_connect`, `sock_send/recv`) return a **Result**,
but the reactor glue reached for the familiar `unwrap()`.

**Correct spelling since v6.6.0** — `Result`, `Option` and `Either` are
`enum ...: stack`, so a payload variant returns `(tag, payload)` in a REGISTER PAIR
and construction allocates zero bytes. Bind both halves:

    var t, v = sock_send(fd, buf, n);
    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return err_code_of(t, v); }
    # v is the value

- ⛔ **`payload()` is DELETED and has no 1-argument replacement** — rdx never reaches
  a parameter. Under the value form the payload is already a plain variable, so
  `payload(r)` becomes `r`. `tagged_new()` is deleted with it.
- Helpers that need the payload gained the tag: `unwrap(t, v)`,
  `unwrap_or(t, v, fallback)`, `option_print(t, v)`, `result_unwrap(t, v)`,
  `result_unwrap_or(t, v, fallback)`, `err_code_of(t, v)`, `result_print(t, v)`.
- Tag-only predicates keep **arity 1** — argument 1 receives rax, which for a pair
  IS the tag: `is_ok`, `is_err_result`, `is_none`, `is_some`, `is_left`, `is_right`,
  `is_tag`, `tag` (now identity).

⛔ **The migration rule has its own silent fail-open, and it cost 19 sites in yukti
alone.** `return res;` on an Err path returns the PAYLOAD by itself, so `Err(77)`
reads downstream as `tag=77, is_err=0` — **an error that reads as success.** Both
halves must be returned: `return (t, v);`. The compiler warns on a mixed-return
function since v6.6.0, but the warning is not a hard error, so read it.

Rule of thumb, unchanged across the flip: `unwrap` is Option-only; if a fn returns
`Result<T,E>`, never `unwrap` it.


[[entries]]
name = "sockaddr_ipv4_is_network_order_bytes_packed_as_int"
description = "async_connect/async_resolve pass IPv4 as the 4 network-order octets packed low-byte-first into an int (127.0.0.1 = 0x0100007F), NOT the host-order a.b.c.d value; resolve→connect round-trips untouched"
category = "cyrius"
---
The `addr` argument to `async_connect(rt, addr, port)` (and the nameserver addr to
`async_resolve`) is the four IPv4 octets in **network byte order, packed low-byte-
first into an integer** — e.g. 127.0.0.1 is `0x0100007F` (b0=127 in the LOW byte),
matching how it is stored raw with `store32(&sa+4, addr)` into `sockaddr_in`. The
`port` is HOST order and byte-swapped inline to network order at the store site.
`async_resolve` returns its resolved A record in the IDENTICAL packed form
(`_async_dns_parse_a` reassembles the 4 answer octets as
`b0 | (b1<<8) | (b2<<16) | (b3<<24)`), so a resolve result feeds STRAIGHT into
`async_connect` with no conversion. The trap: this int is NOT the human-readable
a.b.c.d and NOT host-order — passing `0x7F000001` (host-order 127.0.0.1), or
`htonl`-ing the resolve output before connect, silently connects to the wrong host
with no error. Same convention across net.cyr's sockaddr builders.

[[entries]]
name = "string_literal_to_str_coercion_is_annotation_only"
description = "A bare `\"...\"` coerces to a `Str` struct ONLY when the receiving param is annotated `: Str`. Into an UNTYPED param it arrives as a raw cstr pointer, and any str_* call then reads `load64(ptr+8)` as garbage — a silent empty/zero result, never an error. Shipped undetected for ~a year across 11 lib/fs.cyr fns; fixed v6.4.72."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE RULE: the compiler's string-literal → `Str` auto-coercion
(`parse_fn.cyr`) fires only when BOTH hold —

    1. the argument is a LITERAL token, and
    2. the receiving parameter is annotated `: Str`.

Miss either and you get the raw null-terminated `cstr` pointer.

THE SILENT FAILURE:

    fn find_files(path, ext) {          # UNTYPED params
        ...
        if (str_ends_with(name, ext)) { ... }
    }

    find_files("src", ".cyr");          # returns 0 matches. No error.

  `".cyr"` enters as a cstr pointer, flows unchanged through the
  untyped `ext`, and reaches `str_ends_with`, whose `suffix: Str` IS
  typed — but coercion happens at the CALL BOUNDARY, and by then the
  value is an i64, not a literal token. `str_len(suffix)` does
  `load64(ptr+8)` and reads whatever sits 8 bytes past the string data.
  Every comparison fails. You get an empty result set, a zero count, a
  never-taken branch. **Never a diagnostic.**

WHY THE OBVIOUS TESTS PASS ANYWAY:

    str_ends_with(name, ".cyr")         # WORKS  — literal + `: Str` param
    find_files(path, str_from(".cyr"))  # WORKS  — real Str value
    find_files(path, ".cyr")            # BROKEN — literal + untyped param

  So a direct probe of the leaf function works, and any caller that
  already wrapped in `str_from` works. Only the bare-literal call into
  the untyped API is wrong, which is why it survived ~a year in
  `lib/fs.cyr` (`find_files`, `find_files_with_prunes`, `path_has_ext`
  and 8 siblings) and was found only when `cyrius coverage` reported
  `0/1097` and someone chased it. The working hypothesis at the time
  was allocator aliasing — it was not.

THE FIX IS AT THE API BOUNDARY, NOT THE CALL SITE:

    fn find_files(path: Str, ext: Str) { ... }   # annotate — done

  An `IDENT` argument is never coerced, so `str_from`-wrapped callers
  are byte-identical after the annotation. Annotating is free.

THIS IS NOT A COMPILER BUG. Literal-only coercion is deliberate: a
runtime `i64` cannot be known to be a `Str`. The library simply failed
to express its contract. The lesson is a review rule:

  **Any public fn that takes a string MUST annotate the param `: Str`.**
  An unannotated string param is an API defect, and it fails silently
  rather than loudly — which is the worst possible failure mode for a
  stdlib. Grep your own modules for `fn <name>(...)` with a
  string-shaped parameter and no `: Str`.

CROSS-REF: CHANGELOG [6.4.72]; the same release fixed `cyrius coverage`,
which had been working AROUND this bug with an inline `dir_walk` +
`str_ends_with` filter.

[[entries]]
name = "str_int_cstr_are_reserved_fn_name_suffixes_that_rewrite_your_call_sites"
description = "**`_str`, `_int` and `_cstr` are RESERVED FUNCTION-NAME SUFFIXES in cyrius, not free-form naming.** If `X` and `X_str` both exist, the compiler REWRITES a call `X(a, ...)` into `X_str(a, ...)` whenever argument 1 is `Str`-typed — likewise `X_int` for an i64-returning call argument. **What \"Str-typed\" means, since 6.6.16:** the WHOLE first argument is a Str — a Str local, param or closure capture, a `: Str` or inferred-Str global (`var g = str_from(..)`), a `: Str` field one step past a name or a call (`h.name`, `mkh().name`), or a call or method declared `: Str` (`mk()`, `g<T>(..)`, `s.cat(t)`, `h.getname()`, `mk().clone()`). A call or method result is typed by its OWN declared return, never by its receiver: `s.len()` and `s.data()` are not Str. Two steps (`a.b.name`, `h.name.clone()`) and a generic whose return is `T` (`id<Str>(s)`) are not typed. Before 6.6.16 only a Str LOCAL or param routed, so `println(gs)` on a Str global printed the header's pointer bytes and `strlen(gs)` answered 3; and `x.data`, `x.len`, `x.len()` and `x + 8` on a Str local were routed by the base's type into `X_str`, which read the value as a header (memory-unsafe). An untyped local, param or capture named like a Str global is NOT a Str. The I11 warning (\"passing Str-typed X … which expects a cstring\") uses the same classifier, so a call that can be routed is never warned about. Registration is bidirectional at `REGFN`, so include order does not save you. The mechanism exists for genuine type variants (`println(cstr)` / `println_str(Str)`) and it is invisible until it misfires. **THE RULE: `X_str` must be the Str-taking variant of `X` — a cstr+len form may NEVER occupy that name.** Violating it means calls to `X` silently execute `X_str`. This is not hypothetical: bayan named its cstr+len entries `bayan_json_v_parse_str(buf, len)` alongside `bayan_json_v_parse(src)`, so every `bayan_json_v_parse(someStr)` in the ecosystem became a **1-argument call to a 2-argument function** — `len` bound to garbage, parse returned 0 for every input including valid documents — and the only outward sign was one `expects 2 arguments, got 1` warning line lost in build output. It cost bayan a BREAKING public-API rename (1.3.0, `_str` -> `_buf`) to escape, and sakshi carried the same collision latently (`_sk_write_str/5` shadowing the unrelated `_sk_write/2`). **As of cyrius 6.5.1 the compiler is arity-aware** — it only redirects when the target's parameter count matches the arguments you actually supplied — and a wrong argument count is now a hard ERROR that withholds the binary rather than a warning. **`_int` IS DIFFERENT FROM `_str`, and as of 6.5.2 it has an explicit condition.** The `_int` route only ever fired for a bare FN-CALL first argument — never a literal, never a variable — so `take(v)` and `take(42)` called the base while `take(mk())` ran `take_int`: the same value dispatching differently depending on whether it had passed through a variable. From 6.5.2 the route requires the base's parameter 0 to be explicitly annotated **`: cstring`**, which is the condition it was always approximating (\"this param wants a POINTER, so convert the number for it\"). Practical rule: if you want `X`/`X_int` auto-dispatch, annotate X's first parameter `: cstring`; if you do NOT want it, leave it unannotated and the pair is inert. **Also 6.5.2: passing an integer LITERAL to a `: cstring` parameter is now a hard error** — it used to compile silently and SIGSEGV, since the callee dereferences the number as a `char*` (`println(42)` printed nothing and died on signal 11). `0` remains legal as NULL. An i64 VARIABLE or a fn-call result passed to a `: cstring` param is still only a warning, and always will be: under ADR-002's i64-everywhere tenet those are genuinely indistinguishable from a pointer. A literal is the one case the compiler can actually judge. So the trap is defused on 6.5.1+, but the NAMING rule still stands: pick `_buf` (or anything that is not `_str` / `_int` / `_cstr`) for a pointer+length form, because a suffix that lies about a function's type is confusing even when it is no longer dangerous. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1], bayan CHANGELOG [1.3.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_readable_ms_bounded_wait"
description = "`async_await_readable(fd)` parks on epoll with a hardcoded -1 and returns a constant 0 — a cooperative server parked there cannot be woken to STOP. Use `async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` (v6.5.6): 1 = readable, 0 = timed out."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by sandhi/agnosai while building a graceful-shutdown facility. `async_await_readable(fd)` builds its own epfd, registers `fd`, and calls `sys_epoll_wait(epfd, &revents, 1, 0 - 1)`. Two separate problems live in that one line:

1. **The `-1` is unbounded.** A caller parked there is unwakeable by anything except data on `fd` — no flag, no deadline, no cancellation. Fine for a server whose only job is to serve; fatal for one that must also **stop**. An idle accept loop never observes a shutdown flag set by another thread, because control never leaves the call. A signal handler can fire perfectly and change nothing.
2. **The return value was discarded.** The helper returned a constant `0` regardless of outcome, so even *with* a timeout a caller could not distinguish "the fd is readable" from "the wait expired". A bounded wait that cannot report which happened is not usable.

`async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` fixes both: **1** = readable, **0** = timed out, negative `ms` blocks forever. `async_await_readable` remains as a wrapper returning `0`, so no existing call site moves.

WHAT DOESN'T COVER THIS. `async_with_timeout(rt, handle, ms)` looks like the answer and is not — it races a *spawned task* against a deadline sentinel inside a runtime, whereas this is a bare fd-readiness wait taken on the accept path, outside any task, before a runtime is meaningfully in play.

EINTR. A signal-interrupted wait reports `0`, the same as a timeout. That is deliberate: the caller's loop re-checks its own condition and waits again, which is the correct response to both.

SCOPE. `lib/async.cyr`'s epoll body is gated NOT-agnos AND NOT-win, so this pair is Linux-path only by construction (Windows has `lib/async_win.cyr`, agnos `lib/async_agnos.cyr`). On the **cx** target no syscall peer is included at all, so the body does not even compile there — no cross-target work is owed.

THE TESTING LESSON. Both directions need pinning. A variant hardwired to `1` passes an "it returned instead of hanging" test; a variant hardwired to `0` — the *old* contract — passes that one too. `tests/async_await_readable_ms.sh` asserts an idle fd reports 0 **and** a readable fd reports 1; each mutation fails exactly one axis.

[[entries]]
name = "i64_min_negation_is_a_noop_and_division_truncates_toward_zero"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a NO-OP at i64::MIN (two's-complement abs overflows back to itself), so `n == 0` and `n > 0` are BOTH false and any `while (n > 0)` digit loop runs zero times. That is why every decimal formatter in the stdlib emitted a bare `-` for i64::MIN until v6.5.8. The fix is a sign FLAG + a `!= 0` loop + per-digit negation — which is safe because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on x86, aarch64 AND cx."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — 12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named.

THE ARITHMETIC (all four probed by running the compiler at v6.5.10):

    var m  = 1 << 63;        # i64::MIN
    0 - m  == m              # TRUE  — negation is a NO-OP here
    m > 0                    # FALSE — so a `while (n > 0)` loop never runs
    (0 - 7) / 2 == 0 - 3     # TRUE  — truncates TOWARD ZERO, not floor
    (0 - 7) % 2 == 0 - 1     # TRUE  — remainder takes the DIVIDEND's sign

i64 is two's complement, so the magnitude of `i64::MIN` is one larger
than `i64::MAX` and has no positive representation. `0 - n` overflows
straight back to `n`. There is no error, no trap, no diagnostic.

THE SHAPE THAT BREAKS. Every hand-rolled decimal formatter is written
this way, in every language:

    if (n < 0) { write('-'); n = 0 - n; }    # ← NO-OP at i64::MIN
    if (n == 0) { write('0'); }              # ← skipped, n is not 0
    while (n > 0) { ... }                    # ← skipped, n is not > 0

At `i64::MIN` the sign byte is written and then NOTHING. `fmt_int`
printed `-`. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string
`"-"`, and bayan then serialised `{"n":-}` — not JSON — with no error
raised at any layer.

THE FIX — keep the sign as a FLAG, never negate `n`:

    var neg: i64 = 0;
    if (n < 0) { neg = 1; }
    if (n == 0) { store8(&buf + bi, 48); bi = bi - 1; }
    while (n != 0) {                     # `!= 0`, NOT `> 0`
        var d = n % 10;
        if (d < 0) { d = 0 - d; }        # negate the DIGIT, not n
        store8(&buf + bi, 48 + d); bi = bi - 1; n = n / 10;
    }
    if (neg == 1) { store8(&buf + bi, 45); bi = bi - 1; }

`!= 0` is the load-bearing half. Extracting digits from a negative
value only works because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on **all
three** cyrius backends — that was verified on x86_64, aarch64 and cx
before the fix relied on it, not assumed. (Under floor division
`(0-7)/2` would be `-4` and the digits would come out wrong.)

WHERE IT WAS. Four in `lib/fmt.cyr` (`fmt_int`, `fmt_int_fd`,
`fmt_int_buf`, and a sibling), plus `lib/string.cyr`, `lib/log.cyr`,
sakshi (fixed upstream at 2.4.8 and re-vendored), **two inside the
compiler itself** (`PRNUM`, `_emit_decimal`) that no roadmap entry
listed, and three shipped demos.

⭐ **THE LESSON IS THE HALF-FIX.** `fmt_hex`, sitting directly below
`fmt_int` in the same file, had already been corrected for this exact
`> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at **v6.4.69**. The decimal siblings were never
brought along. When you fix a loop-bound predicate in one formatter,
grep the file for every other `while (n > 0)` before you close it.

REVIEW RULE for your own code: any `while (x > 0)` over an i64 that
came from user data is suspect, and any `x = 0 - x` "take the absolute
value" is a bug at exactly one input. Test formatters and parsers at
`1 << 63`, not just at `-1`.

[[entries]]
name = "arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid"
description = "An exhausted arena returns 0 from `arena_alloc` / `str_from_a` — correct behaviour — but a `Str` of 0 is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a valid `Str`, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and faults layers away. v6.5.9 adds a per-arena exhaustion POLICY (ARENA_FULL_NULL / GROW / SPILL / ABORT) precisely because returning 0 correctly was never enough."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.9** — filed from agnosai.

THE PRIMITIVES WERE ALREADY RIGHT, AND THAT WAS THE PROBLEM.
`arena_alloc` returns 0 when the chunk cannot serve the request,
`str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. None of that is the
bug. The bug is what the 0 does next: **a `Str` of 0 is
indistinguishable from a valid `Str`** — there is no option type, and
the `_a` (allocator-threaded) families have no error channel — so it
flows onward and the first thing that dereferences it faults. "Arena
too small" was observable as a SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON
walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.

⭐ **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MAKES IT SERIOUS.** Before
threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on
the no-free global bump. After threading — a change whose entire
purpose is to stop leaking — the same response is a *crash*. Adopting
the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter. A
5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are
small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of
items, and nothing about that shape probes the ceiling.

THE POLICY (one field on the arena header, set at creation or later):

    ARENA_FULL_NULL  = 0    return 0 — TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, still the
                            default, so no existing arena moves
    ARENA_FULL_GROW  = 1    chain another chunk
    ARENA_FULL_SPILL = 2    serve overflow from the global allocator
                            (weaker — spilled bytes are never reclaimed
                            by reset — but the crash becomes the
                            pre-arena cost)
    ARENA_FULL_ABORT = 3    die loudly AT the allocation instead of
                            faulting three layers away

    var a  = arena_new_growable(65536);        # policy = GROW
    var al = arena_allocator_growable(65536);  # the allocator-interface form
    arena_set_on_full(a, ARENA_FULL_ABORT);    # or change it later
    arena_on_full(a)                           # read it back, -1 if a == 0

⚠ **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`.** The bump allocator
underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible — and
it is not what an arena wants anyway. Reset rewinds to the FIRST chunk
and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water
mark and then allocates nothing. Use `arena_capacity_total(a)` for the
whole chain; `arena_used(a)` cannot show it once the arena has grown.

⚠ `arena_free(a)` used to clear only base/ptr/end and LEAVE THE POLICY
SET, so a "freed" growable arena would take the grow path and succeed.
Fixed in the same release — but if you are on an older toolchain, do
not reuse a freed arena handle.

PRACTICAL RULE: if you cannot bound the allocation from the input,
either use `arena_new_growable` and stop guessing a capacity, or set
`ARENA_FULL_ABORT` so a wrong guess is a diagnosable death rather than
a mystery segfault in whatever code touched the pointer next. Sizing an
arena and hoping is the shape this entry exists to stop.

[[entries]]
name = "thread_is_done_is_valid_only_before_thread_join"
description = "`thread_is_done(t)` (v6.5.8) is a non-blocking poll of the handle `thread_create` returned — valid ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`, because join CONSUMES the handle (on Windows it CloseHandle()s it) and a post-join query reports \"not done\" forever. A detached thread has no handle at all, by design."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — the reaper primitives.

    var t = thread_create(&worker, arg);
    while (thread_is_done(t) == 0) { ... keep serving ... }
    thread_join(t);                     # then join once — and STOP asking

`thread_join` blocks unconditionally, which made a reaper — "collect
whichever workers have finished, keep serving the rest" — impossible to
write without a detached thread plus a side channel. `thread_is_done`
reads the same `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` slot `thread_join`'s loop already
tests, so it adds no new state: **1** = done, **0** = still running,
**-1** = null handle.

⛔ **VALID ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`.** Join CONSUMES the handle — on
Windows it `CloseHandle()`s it — so a post-join query inspects a closed
handle and reports "not done", forever. That is not a bug to work
around; after joining, the thread is finished by definition and there is
nothing left to ask. Caught on real cass hardware, by a test that
asserted the wrong contract.

⛔ **A DETACHED THREAD HAS NO HANDLE.** `thread_create_detached(fp,
arg)` returns 1/0 (spawned or not), not a handle, and there is nothing
to poll or join — the child unmaps its own stack in the trampoline tail
and the TLS block is carved from the top of that same mapping so one
`munmap` frees both. Use it for fire-and-forget work; use
`thread_create` + `thread_is_done` + `thread_join` when you need to know
it finished. (Before .8 the only fire-and-forget shape was an unjoined
`thread_create`, which leaked **2,101,248 bytes of VA per thread** —
100 unjoined threads = 210,124,800 B, now 0.)

⚠ RELATED, SAME RELEASE: `thread_join` itself had a lost-wakeup
deadlock — it read the tid word TWICE per iteration, once for the loop
condition and once as the `FUTEX_WAIT` expected value, so a worker
exiting between the two loads let the kernel's one-shot wake fire with
nobody parked and the joiner then parked on an address that would never
be woken again. Permanent, silent, log-less, ~1 join in 150k, SIGKILL
the only recovery. Fixed in v6.5.8. If you are on ≤6.5.7 and a
long-running server wedges in a join, this is it.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_value_return_binding_forms"
description = "A value-form vector return (f32v4/f64v2/f64v4) used to survive only `var r: <exact type> = f(...)`; every other binding form silently read back zero"
category = "cyrius"
---
Until v6.5.61, a function returning a SIMD vector by value was stored correctly by
exactly ONE spelling. The others compiled clean, exited 0, and produced wrong numbers.

    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(a, b);    # worked
    var r        = f64v2_add(a, b);    # lanes read back 0   (no annotation)
    var r: f64v2;  r = f64v2_add(a,b); # lanes read back 0   (assignment form)
    var r: f64v4 = f64v2_add(a, b);    # lanes read back 0   (mismatched type)

Every width was affected — f32v4 returned garbage, f64v2 and f64v4 returned 0 — and
it was never confined to lib/simd.cyr: a user-defined `fn mk(): f64v2` had the same
problem. The most visible symptom was `f64v2_scale(a, f64_from(3))` coming back as
`lo=0, hi=3`, with the SCALE FACTOR sitting in lane 1.

WHY. The 16/32-byte receive paths in PARSE_VAR were gated on the variable's own type
annotation, so a variable without one fell through to the ordinary scalar
initialiser. The assignment path had no vector receive at all and used the generic
8-byte store. Since v6.5.61 the type is inferred from the callee's recorded return
type when the variable declares none, the assignment form has its own receive, and an
annotation that DISAGREES with the callee is a hard error rather than a silent
truncation:

    error: vector return type does not match the declared type
    error: vector return type does not match the assigned variable

WHAT TO TAKE FROM IT. If you are writing SIMD code on a cyrius older than 6.5.61,
annotate every vector binding with its exact type and never use the assignment form.
On 6.5.61+ all forms work, and a wrong annotation is refused instead of silently
dropping lanes.

The reason this survived for so long is worth more than the bug: every call site in
the tests, the guide and the stdlib spelled the annotation, so the API's most natural
spelling was exercised by nothing. The differential corpus was 406/407 byte-identical
across the fix — which measures the corpus, not the fix. When you add a receive path
for a type, test the binding forms a CONSUMER writes, not the one the stdlib writes;
and note that a declaration receive and an assignment receive are two halves of one
statement, not two features (v6.5.57 fixed the same split for structs).

[[entries]]
name = "public_marker_leaks_onto_next_declaration_v664"
description = "A `public` on a struct/union/enum/impl/use in a private file re-exposed the NEXT fn or var (v6.5.0–6.6.3); the marker is now armed only for items that can carry it (v6.6.4)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYMPTOM. A `private` file declares `public enum AdPowLimit { … }` and, right after
it, `fn _ad_pow()`. From another file `_ad_pow()` compiles and runs; every other
`_`-named item in the file is refused. hisab's public-surface gate found exactly ONE
leak among 457 probes (456 refused), and the one was the declaration after the enum.

MECHANISM. `public` set a running marker (`_pub_pending`) that was consumed by the
next fn or global-var DEFINITION — and by nothing else. An enum records no visibility
(enum constants are public by design), so it never touched the marker; the marker
outlived the enum and the next fn or var was stamped public. The same for a bare
`public struct` / `union` / `impl` / `use`, and for `public var a[N]` (the array path
never called the stamp helper). The fix is a POSITIVE list: `public` arms the marker
only when the next token is `var`/`secret`, `fn`/`async`, or a fn-attribute directive;
otherwise it is consumed on the spot.

THE SECOND LAYER. The compiler makes two passes over top-level tokens. Pass 2 skips
`var` inline without consuming the marker and stamps only what pass 1 could not — impl
methods and fns after top-level code — so `public var V = 7;` followed by an impl block
re-exposed the impl's FIRST method even with pass 1 correct. Now PARSE_IMPL and the
relaxed-ordering fn path clear a stale marker on entry. Consequence: `public impl`
marks NO method; write `public fn` per method.

THREE THINGS THE FIX EXPOSED. (1) `public var a, b = f();` stamped `b` private (the
helper cleared the marker after `a`) — one `public` now covers every name. (2) Derived
codecs (`T_to_json`, `T_from_json`, `T_from_json_str`, enum codecs) were emitted
without the type's `public`; only the v6.6.3 accessors carried it, so closing the leak
would have turned "one codec reachable by accident" into "none" — they inherit it
now. (3) The once-per-translation-unit `_cy_enum_name_eq` helper was private to
whichever file derived an enum first; a second private file's enum codec was refused.

THE PATTERN. A "next item" marker that only some declaration kinds consume is an
armed trap; every kind that does NOT consume it is a leak site. Enumerate the kinds
(struct, union, enum, impl, use, mod, array var, destructure var, the pass-2 skip)
and gate each by name — the filing's matrix had tested struct only through
`#derive(accessors)`, whose generated `public fn` happened to consume the marker,
so the struct row read green for the wrong reason.

[[entries]]
name = "private_applies_to_the_whole_file_and_forward_references_are_checked"
description = "`private` scopes to the FILE it appears in, wherever in that file the marker sits — including definitions written above it, and including globals — and a reference that comes earlier in the concatenated stream than the definition it names is enforced exactly like one that comes after. Before 6.6.5 both halves were false, and the consequences ran in BOTH directions: reachable private methods on one side, refused public ones on the other."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["language", "visibility", "private", "include-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Two rules, both true since 6.6.5 and neither true before it:

**1. The marker's POSITION in the file does not matter.** `private`
alone on a line flips the whole file. A fn declared ABOVE that line is
file-private like any other, and so is a global. Until 6.6.5 the flip
was recorded as the compiler's first pass walked past the marker, so
anything declared earlier was stamped as if the file were public — and
a global above the line was never private in EITHER include order.

**2. A FORWARD reference is checked.** If the call is parsed before the
definition — the library included BELOW the code that uses it — the
boundary still applies: `q.m()` and `Q_m(&q)` on an impl method,
`a + b` through an operator `impl`, a `mod`-scoped fn, a fn defined
after the first top-level statement. Until 6.6.5 every one of those
compiled and RAN (exit 42 on the filed repro).

⚠ **THE MIRROR IMAGE IS THE PART WORTH KNOWING, because it bit code
that did nothing unusual.** The same root made the compiler keep one
symbol-table entry per NAME during that first pass, so:

* Two files that each declare `private` and each define a helper of the
  same name got a FALSE `'_h' is private to its file` — and a false
  `'_h' expects 2 arguments, got 1` when the arities differed.
* A public fn could inherit a private stamp from an unrelated file's
  same-named private one. The language guide's own two-file `_helper`
  example was REFUSED when the public file was included first.
* A forward call passing a plain struct larger than 8 bytes SIGSEGV'd —
  silently, with no diagnostic, on Linux, macOS/aarch64 and Windows.

**If you are on a toolchain before 6.6.5, the workaround for the
fail-open is the normal include order (library first) — but note that
the SAME order is what triggers the false-refusal half, so there is no
ordering that avoids both. Pin 6.6.5 or later.**

There is also no per-item `private`: `private fn h()` is a hard error
naming the file-level form (v6.5.56). ⚠ Until 6.6.5 that error printed
TWICE and privatised the file anyway, so a legitimate sibling fn in the
same file was then reported `is private to its file` at ITS caller —
the diagnostic pointed at correct code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_struct_declared_inside_a_fn_is_per_call_and_per_thread"
description = "Until v6.6.5 a struct LITERAL written inside a fn (`var p = T { .. }`) was compiled as a GLOBAL: shared across recursion, shared across threads, and visible to every later reference of that name in ANY file of the program. From v6.6.5 it is a per-call frame object like `var p: T;` has always been. The rules a consumer needs: a struct declared inside a fn is per call and per thread; a struct declared at top level is one shared object; and `var p: T = <expr>` where the expression yields an ADDRESS is a POINTER to someone else's struct, not a copy."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "storage", "locals", "recursion", "threads", "scoping", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**What changed, in consumer terms.** These three now mean what they look like:

```cyrius
fn f(n) {
    var a = P { n, n * 2 };   # per call, per thread (was a GLOBAL before v6.6.5)
    var b: P;                 # per call, per thread (always was)
    var c: P = mk();          # a POINTER to whatever mk() returned — NOT a copy
    return a.x + b.y;
}
```

**Symptoms you would have seen before v6.6.5**, all silent: a recursive fn building a literal
returned the innermost call's value; N threads running the same fn scribbled over one another;
and a `var cfg = P { 1, 2 };` inside any fn of any file captured a top-level `var cfg = 5;`
elsewhere in the program. The last one needed no `private`, no second file and no collision the
author could see — just the same identifier twice.

**Two behaviours that follow, and are now consistent.** Operator overloading dispatches for a
struct VALUE wherever it lives — literal local, typed local, annotated local, global — and does
NOT dispatch for a pointer-mode local, where `+` is pointer arithmetic. And a closure that
references an enclosing struct or array captures it BY VALUE, including a stack array through
`&buf`, which previously failed to compile.

**If you relied on the old behaviour** — a literal that persisted across calls — declare it at
top level. That is what it actually was.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_over_budget_array_local_is_static_storage_and_says_so"
description = "`var buf[N]` inside a fn is per-thread STACK storage, but past the per-fn frame budget (~120 KB of slots) it falls back to ONE shared static buffer. As of v6.6.5 the compiler says so with a `warning:` rather than a `note:`, and the name is scoped to its fn so it can no longer collide with a global elsewhere in the program. `CYRIUS_STACK_ARRAYS=0` puts EVERY array local on that path."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arrays", "storage", "threads", "secret", "diagnostics", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
```
warning: array local over the per-fn frame budget gets STATIC storage: one buffer shared by
all calls and all threads; use alloc() for per-call storage
```

**Read it as a concurrency warning, not a size warning.** The buffer works; it is simply not
per call and not per thread. If the fn can be re-entered or run on two threads, move the
buffer to `alloc()`. Two known consumers (a 256 KB crypto scratch and a test server buffer)
bank theirs deliberately and are correct as they stand.

**Before v6.6.5** the same declaration ALSO took a program-wide global name, so it captured
every later reference to that identifier in any file (measured: 7 where 3 was right) and, from
v6.6.4, produced a visibility error naming the CONSUMER's variable. It was invisible to the
local table too, so an enclosing local of the same name won inside its own fn and `&buf` wrote
into that scalar. And `secret var buf[N]` over the budget was refused outright — or, worse,
compiled and never zeroised. All four are closed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_top_level_block_scopes_its_vars_since_6_6_6"
description = "A `var` declared inside a top-level block (an `if`/`while`/`for`/`switch` body outside every fn) belongs to that block and is gone at its `}` — the same rule as a fn body, since 6.6.6. Before that it registered a GLOBAL: the name stayed visible after the block AND an inner declaration of an outer name overwrote the outer global (measured 2 where 1 is correct). Reading one after its block is now an error that names the variable."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["scope", "globals", "top-level", "semantics", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The rule.** These two are now the same language:

    fn f(): i64 { var c = 1; if (c == 1) { var t = 5; } return t; }   # undefined variable 't'
    var c = 1; var x = 0; x = 1;
    if (c == 1) { var t = 5; }
    syscall(60, t);                                                   # undefined variable 't'

Before 6.6.6 the second one compiled and exited 5. A top-level `var` inside a block went
through `PARSE_VAR`'s `GINFN == 0` arm and registered a global, and the global var table had
no scope mechanism at all — `SCOPE_POP` cleared only the frame-local table.

**The part that was worse than visibility.** With 6.6.6's one-global-per-name fold, an inner
declaration of an OUTER name did not shadow it, it *was* it:

    var limit = 1;
    var go = 0; go = 1;
    if (go == 1) { var limit = 2; }
    syscall(60, limit);       # 2 on x86 before 6.6.6; 1 now

cx and aarch64 already answered 1, so the self-host fixpoint and both cross-target legs were
green over an x86-only wrong answer. When two targets disagree about a *frontend* rule, the
frontend is where to look.

**Writing code that needs the value after the block.** Declare above, assign inside —
assignment to an outer global from inside a block is unchanged, only DECLARATIONS are scoped:

    var t = 0;
    if (go == 1) { t = 5; }
    syscall(60, t);           # 5

**Recognising it.** The error names the variable and says where to put it:

    note: 't' was declared inside a top-level block and goes out of scope at its '}'
          (since 6.6.6 a top-level block scopes its `var`s like a fn body does)
          declare it at top level, before the block, to use it after the block

A survey of 12,604 `.cyr` sources across the ecosystem when the change landed found no file
that relied on the leak, so nothing downstream had to change — but code written against
pre-6.6.6 cyrius that does rely on it fails loudly rather than quietly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "println_dispatches_on_the_declared_return_type"
description = "A helper declared `: i64` that returns a string pointer gets println() rerouted to the NUMERIC printer — silently"
category = "cyrius"
---
`println` is an overload-dispatch target (lib/string.cyr, v5.10.5): PARSE_FNCALL routes
`println(x)` to `println_int` whenever the argument is i64-TYPED — an `: i64`-annotated
local or global, or the result of an `: i64`-annotated fn call. The dispatch reads the
DECLARATION, not what the value actually points at.

So a perfectly ordinary helper:

    fn _suite_row(idx, run): i64 {        # every helper in this codebase is `: i64`
        if (idx == 0) { return "selfhost"; }
        ...
    }
    println(_suite_row(i, 0));            # prints 6551721, not "selfhost"

prints nine pool ADDRESSES where you wanted nine names. No diagnostic, exit 0 — the
program is type-correct as far as the compiler is concerned, because `: i64` is exactly
what you told it. Measured 6.6.6 (bite 27a) on the check driver's `--list-suites`.

WHAT TO DO. Either annotate the helper honestly (`: cstring`), or print through a
cstring-only primitive rather than the overloaded name — the check driver already has
`_p(cstr)` for exactly this and the fix was `_p(name); _p("\n");`.

⚠ THE SHAPE, not the symbol: any overload-dispatched stdlib name behaves this way, and
`: i64` is this codebase's default annotation for *everything* (ADR-002), so the two
collide constantly. When a stdlib call prints or formats the wrong KIND of thing, suspect
the declared return type of whatever produced the argument before you suspect the callee.

[[entries]]
name = "float_negation_is_a_sign_flip_since_6_6_8"
description = "Before cycc 6.6.8, `-1.0` evaluated to -4.0 and `-x` on an f64 negated the BIT PATTERN; f64_to disagreed across targets"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.8 compiled unary minus on a float LITERAL or an f64-typed operand as INTEGER
negation of its bit pattern: `-1.0` is `0 - 0x3FF0000000000000`, which reads back as
-4.0, and `-0.0` came out +0. x86 `f64_neg(+0)` also gave +0. No warning. Consumers carried
workaround rules (dhvani, ganita) or, like ranga, wrote `-4.0` meaning `-1.0`.

SINCE 6.6.8: unary minus on a float literal folds to `bits ^ (1 << 63)`, on an F64-typed
operand it emits a sign flip, and x86 `f64_neg` is `btc rax, 63`. `f64_to` agrees on every
target: NaN -> 0, out-of-range saturates (x86 used to return 0x8000000000000000).

STILL INTEGER, BY DESIGN (ADR-002 — values are i64 unless typed): unary minus on an
UNTYPED variable that happens to hold float bits. Type it (`var x: f64`) or call
`f64_neg`. A kind-2 warning now flags `int_expr OP f64_expr` mixes, which is how ranga's
`-4.0` was found. ⚠ Open at 6.6.8: f64 struct fields and f64 builtin RESULTS are not yet
F64-typed, so arithmetic and unary minus on them are still integer (6.6.8 review finds).

[[entries]]
name = "exp_ln_family_one_ulp_contract_since_6_6_8"
description = "f64_exp/exp2/ln/log2 are <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every target since cycc 6.6.8 — not bit-identical across targets"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.8's aarch64 polyfills (no x87 there) returned finite values for NaN / negative /
zero inputs to f64_ln and drifted by many ulp (tyche's golden stream caught it); PE's x87
f64_exp ran at 53-bit precision control. SINCE 6.6.8: fdlibm ports on aarch64 and cx, range
guards BEFORE x*log2e, every negative input -> NaN, +0 -> -inf, and PE saves/sets/restores
the x87 control word around exp/exp2. The PROMISE is <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every
target — NOT bit-identical results across targets; compare with a ulp bar, never `==`.
sin/cos/atan get the same treatment in 6.6.9.

[[entries]]
name = "trig_one_ulp_and_x86_off_fsin_since_6_6_9"
description = "f64_sin/cos/atan are <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every target since cycc 6.6.9; x86 no longer uses bare x87 fsin/fcos"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.9: the aarch64/cx polyfills carried a 1e-9 ABSOLUTE tolerance (hundreds of ulp near
zeros of sin/cos), and x86 used bare x87 `fsin`/`fcos`, whose argument reduction uses a 66-bit pi
and loses every bit of accuracy past ~2^63 (and is visibly wrong near k*pi/2 well before that).
SINCE 6.6.9: fdlibm ports (Cody-Waite + __kernel_rem_pio2, k_sin/k_cos with y_lo, s_atan with an
exact +/-pi/4) on every target, and x86 / PE / x86-Mach-O call that same software routine — at a
DSP-speed cost. The promise matches exp/ln's: <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials, NOT bit-identical across
targets; compare with a ulp bar.

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_call_refused_whatever_its_shape_since_6_6_9"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.9 a REACHABLE call to an undefined fn fails the build on every target and in every position — including a tail call on aarch64"
category = "cyrius"
---
Before 6.6.9 the x86 forks refused a reachable undefined call, but an aarch64 build let one through
when it sat in TAIL position (`return missing(x);`), shipping a binary that crashed there; four of
the seven forks also skipped the pre-pass that WARNS about undefined calls. Since 6.6.9 one top-level
directive dispatcher serves all seven forks, every fork runs the pre-pass, and a reachable undefined
call is refused whatever its shape. A call from a fn DCE proves dead is still only a warning.
Related 6.6.9 diagnostics: a struct/union re-declared with a DIFFERENT layout warns (the first
layout used to win silently), an enum member and a top-level var of the same name with different
values warn, and bare `#deprecated` needs its message. Since 6.6.11 a `#derive`d struct
re-declared with different field names, order or size is an ERROR, because its generated
accessors would address the first layout's bytes.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_types_checked_and_f64_fields_typed_since_6_6_10"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 an f64/f32 struct field loads typed, a struct value assigned into a struct-typed field copies the whole struct, and an unknown / forward / self field type is a compile error; u8..u64 and f32 fields are 8 bytes"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, all silent: (1) `x: f64` was not in the field-type ladder, so it registered as
an i64 and every operator on `p.x` was an INTEGER op on the bits (`p.x + p.y` = NaN for
1.5 + 2.0, `p.x * p.y` = 0, `p.x < p.y` wrong for negatives), through a local, a parameter, a
global, a chain, a union, a method's self and Pair<f64>; correct code (`2.0 * p.x`) drew a false
kind-1 warning. (2) `b.v = p` into a struct-typed field stored ONE word (from an address-passed
struct parameter, the pointer). (3) A field type naming nothing (`a: Nonexist`), a struct used
above its declaration (`struct A { b: B; }` before B — A got a 16-byte layout instead of 24),
`v: i16v8` (registered as a 2-byte i16) all compiled; `struct Node { next: Node; }` crashed cycc.
SINCE 6.6.10: the field load types F64/F32 (a per-field float-kind side table, no layout change);
the field store copies the whole struct from a name, a call / method / operator result or another
field, and refuses a different struct type by name — since 6.6.11 also a <= 8 B method /
operator result, which arrives in rax untyped and was not checked at 6.6.10 (the reverse
direction, `q = b.v` / `var p: P3 = b.v`, copies whole since 6.6.11 too: see
language/features.cyml `struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611`); an unknown type is
refused by name, a forward one with "declare it before 'A'", a vector and a self-reference by
name.
⚠ AN ODD-SIZED FIELD (3/5/6/7 B, e.g. `struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }`) took the store ladder's
8-byte arm for any value in rax — a <= 8 B method / operator result, a top-level call, a plain
integer — and silently overwrote the fields AFTER it (on every backend and real host). Since
6.6.10 such a store writes exactly the field's bytes (4/2/1-byte chunks, shifted into place).
Width ladders that fall to "else: 8 bytes" are the recurring shape: an odd size is not 8.
⚠ WIDTHS ARE UNCHANGED: u8/u16/u32/u64 and f32 fields are 8 bytes each (only i8/i16/i32 are
narrow) — `struct { a: u8; b: u8; }` is 16 bytes. For a binary layout use i8/i16/i32 + mask.
⚠ `#derive(accessors)` getters are `load64` and stay UNTYPED on purpose (bit-exact getter
compares in svara/samay): `p.x + p.y` is a float add, `P_x(&p) + P_y(&p)` an integer one.


[[entries]]
name = "float_builtin_results_are_float_operands_since_6_6_10"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 + - * / on a float builtin's result is float arithmetic (option D: a result flag, not a type); compares on builtin results stay integer; unary minus on an untyped float-initialised var and an integer constant into an f64/f32 slot warn"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, silent: a float-returning builtin (f64_sqrt, f64_add, f64_sin, f32_from, ...)
left the estype of its LAST ARGUMENT, 0 under the i64-boxed idiom, so every + - * / on its
result was INTEGER arithmetic on the bits: `f64_sqrt(u) * f64_sqrt(u)` = 0,
`f64_sin(z) - f64_cos(z)` = -4.0, `f64_mul(u, v) / v` = 1, `-(f64_sqrt(v))` the integer
negation, `-f64_sqrt(u) * 2.0` an integer multiply — and `f64_add(p, u) * 2.0` differed from
`f64_add(u, p) * 2.0` because the argument ORDER decided the type.
SINCE 6.6.10: the result is a float operand of + - * / on either side, directly, in parens
and after unary minus, whatever the arguments were typed. It is a transient flag (code pointer
+ token index at the end of the call), NOT a type: ⚠ `==` / `<` on builtin results are still
INTEGER compares of the bits (41 ecosystem sites rely on that — hex constants, hisab's -0
check, ganita's `f64_floor(v) == v`); `f64_neg(a) < f64_neg(b)` is wrong for negatives — use
f64_lt/f64_gt/f64_eq. `var x = f64_sqrt(u);` is still UNTYPED (declare `: f64`). Code that
wants the integer ulp distance of two results must go through an untyped temp first:
`var a = f64_atan(x); d = a - b;` (cyrius's own exp2_atan_bigtrig.tcyr had to).
New warnings (CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0 silences them): `var c = 1.5; -c` is integer negation
(-3.0; `-z` of 0.0 is +0, `-n` of -2.5 is 1.75) → "unary minus on an untyped variable holding
a float is integer negation (declare it f64)" — judged at the DECLARATION (float literal, f64
value, float builtin, or a copy of such a var) and, since 6.6.11, RE-JUDGED by every plain
assignment: `var g = 0; g = 1.5; -g` warns and `var h = 1.5; h = 7; -h` does not, for locals
and globals alike (a compound `h += ..` keeps the judgement). Not flow-sensitive: the last
assignment the compiler READ wins — source order in a fn, and for a global source order across
fns. (6.6.11 also TYPES a pure copy of a declared float, `var t = p.y;` / `var u = y;`, so
that one is a float, not a warned untyped var — see language/features.cyml
`f64_named_type_and_operators`.) And
`var t: f64 = 1;` / `t = 1;` / `p.x = 1;` / `P { 1, 2 }` store 0x1 (a subnormal) → "an
integer stored into an f64/f32 slot keeps its integer bits"; only compile-time integer
constants are judged, 0 and hex bit patterns >= 2^52 are exempt, a runtime untyped value
(`var x: f64 = load64(p)`) is the legal boxed idiom and is not.
Ecosystem at landing: 0 binary or diagnostic changes over 6,226 non-vendored files.


[[entries]]
name = "generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 a generic that uses a T-typed value as a struct (`p.y`) has no i64 or other scalar instance: every scalar call, tail call, &g and forwarding generic is a compile error naming it; struct inference binds the struct instance on every call path"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, silent: `fn g<T>(p: T) { .. p.y .. }` has no valid i64 form, so its BASE (the
i64 instance) is emitted as a `rax = 0` dead stub — and that decision was made only while the
base was being emitted, recorded nowhere. So `g<i64>(5)`, `g(5)`, `return g(5);`, `&g` (then
fncall1) and `outer<i64>(5)` with `fn outer<T>(p: T) { return g<T>(p) + 2; }` all compiled
and returned 0 (outer: 2); an inlinable body said "no struct type in scope for 'p'" instead.
Worse, the STRUCT calls ran the stub too: struct inference existed only in the inline replay
(positionally, re-typing the param i64), so `var r = g(p)`, `return g(p)`, `g(mk(2))`,
`g(2, p)` (T on param 1), `g(q)` with `q: Pt` a parameter and `outer<Pt>(p)` forwarding
`g(p)` all returned 0 — only the explicit `g<Pt>(p)` worked.
SINCE 6.6.10: pass 1 records, BY NAME, each generic's T-typed names (params + `var q: T`
locals), whether one is used as a struct (`x.f`; `n..m` is a range), and which param carries
each type parameter; a fixpoint marks a generic that forwards a T-typed value or T itself into
such a generic. Every scalar use of one is refused: "generic 'g' has no i64 (or other scalar)
instance: it uses its type parameter as a struct ...". A struct argument (local, param,
global, struct-returning call) instantiates `g$Pt` exactly like `g<Pt>(p)` on every path —
expression, var init, tail (`return g(p)` leaves the tail path ONLY for such a callee: a
scalar generic keeps its tail call, a 20M-deep `cnt<T>` must not overflow), argument, struct
receive, `r = g(p);`, `b.v = g(p);`. Only a WHOLE argument (a bare name, one whole call)
binds T — reading the argument's first token made `f(p.y)` bind Pt and fail on an undefined
`Pt_add`; a `pp: *Pt` parameter is a pointer and binds i64. Two inferred struct type args
(or a struct beside a scalar) are refused like the explicit `g<Pt, Q>`, on the paths that
emit their OWN call too (the resolver used to swallow the failure and run the base). A
signature naming `Box<Pt>` / `Box<T>` is the instance, not the base, and an instance's `: T`
return with T a struct returns that struct (it returned its address).
⚠ Every path that emits its own call must resolve the callee (`_gen_resolve_call`), not
FINDFN the base — the first 6.6.10 cut missed the register-pair return check and the
assignment paths, and each miss was silent or a regression.
⚠ The stub scan is lexical, so it errs toward MORE stubs — a loud refusal, never a silent 0.

[[entries]]
name = "closure_returns_belong_to_the_closure_since_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a `return f(..)` inside a closure body (`|x| { .. }`, `|| { .. }`) is the closure's own: a fn that builds such a closure is no longer treated as returning a `: stack` pair, refused 'bind both' at its callers, or warned for its own plain return — and a closure that mixes a pair return with a plain one is warned as its own fn"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.6.0 - 6.6.15, wrong diagnostics: with `fn h(x) { return Ok(x); }`,

    fn mk(b) { var g = |x| { return h(x + b); }; return g; }   # returns ONE value: the closure
    fn main() { var g = mk(41); return 0; }                    # error: "bind both"

was refused, because the two post-parse scans that find pair-returning fns
(`_pair_prescan`, `_warn_mixed_pair_returns` in src/frontend/parse.cyr) skipped a nested
body only at a `fn` token, and a closure literal has none — so the closure's
`return h(..)` was booked to `mk`. Consequences: every single-variable bind of `mk(..)`
inside a fn was a hard error (top level: a warning), the flag propagated through
forwarding wrappers, `mk`'s own `return g;` drew "returns a `: stack` pair on another path
but a SINGLE value here", a real pair fn whose closure said `return 0;` was warned, and a
real mixed-return fn was warned at the closure's return instead of its own.
⛔ Following the error's hint was WRONG CODE: `var t, v = mk(41);` compiled (warning only)
and `v` was a stale rdx — `fncall1(v, 1)` exited 139. The workaround hisab shipped (3.3.1+)
is still valid: bind both halves inside the closure and `return 0;`.

SINCE 6.6.16: both scans skip a closure body — a `{` immediately after `|` or `||` (exact:
closure params are untyped with no return annotation, and `{` never starts an expression).
The closure's OWN pair-ness is untouched: its body parses as its own fn, so
`var t, v = fncall1(g, 1)` on a closure that returns `Ok(..)` gets both halves. A brace-less
closure (`|x| h(x)`) never had the problem (an expression has no `return`).

A CLOSURE IS A FN FOR THE MIXED-RETURN RULE. Each closure body is judged as its own unit, at
any depth: if its own body (nested closures and fns skipped) says `return f(..)` or `f(..)?`
with f a pair fn, every other return must be a variant too, or the first plain one is warned
"a closure in `mk` returns a `: stack` pair on another path but a SINGLE value here" ("a
closure returns ..." at top level / in an impl method). So

    var g = |x| { if (x > 0) { return h(x); } return 0; };    # warned at `return 0;`
    var t, v = fncall1(g, 0);                                   # v would be a stale rdx

Return `Err(..)` (or `None()` for an Option) on that path. `return None();` beside
`return Some(x);` is a whole value and stays silent, as in a fn.

⚠ For compiler work: (1) skipping a nested body is HALF the job — whatever skips a unit must
also judge it as its own, or the skip silently deletes the only diagnostic that unit had (the
first cut of this fix did exactly that; the gate's mutation ledger row c pins it). (2) If
closure params or a return type ever gain annotations (`|x: i64|: R { .. }`),
`_tok_is_closure_body` must scan the param list instead of looking one token back — the
closure parser (parse_expr.cyr) carries a comment saying so.
Gate: tests/gates/frontend/stack_enum_closure_return_scope.sh. CHANGELOG [6.6.16]

[[entries]]
name = "pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 an explicit generic call `g<T>(..)` and a method call `p.m(..)` are the same call as `f(..)` for a `: stack` pair: `?` propagates it, `var a, b = ..` destructures it, and a single bind / assignment / store of it is refused. Before that they were not recognised: `?` exited 139 and a single bind dropped the payload silently."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.6.15 and older, x86 and aarch64 (Result is the `: stack` value form since 6.6.0):

    fn gdiv<T>(n: T, d: T) { if (d == 0) { return Err(99); } return Ok(n / d); }
    struct Pt { x; y; }
    fn Pt_div(self: Pt, d) { if (d == 0) { return Err(99); } return Ok(self.x / d); }

    fn a(n, d) { var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d)?; return Ok(v); }   # rc 139
    fn b(p: Pt, d) { var v = p.div(d)?; return Ok(v); }      # rc 139
    fn c(n, d) { var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d); return v; }        # compiled; Ok(3) read 0 (payload gone)
    fn e(n, d) { var t, v = gdiv<i32>(n, d); }               # "multi-value destructure needs a call"
    fn g(p: Pt, d) { p.div(d)?; return Ok(0); }              # "expected ';', got unknown"

The plain spellings `pdiv(n, d)?` / `Pt_div(p, d)?` were right, and `var v = pdiv(n, d);` was refused.

WHY: every place that asks "does this call return a pair" — `?`'s lowering choice, the lossy-bind refusals,
`return`'s wrapper propagation, the destructure, and the forward-reference prescan — tested for `IDENT (`
only. `g<i32>(` has a `<` there and `p.div(` a `.`, so all of them answered "no pair": `?` took the BOXED
lowering, which dereferences its operand, and read the tag (0 or 1) as a pointer.

SINCE 6.6.16 one resolver answers for every asker, in the three spellings: `name(`, `name<..>(` (the generic
template carries the pair flag) and `x.m(` with x a single name whose struct is known (a local, a closure's
capture, or a struct global — the order the method call itself resolves it in). So:

    var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d)?;     var v = p.div(d)?;     p.div(d)?;      # propagate both halves
    var t, v = gdiv<i32>(n, d);   var t, v = p.div(d);                   # in a fn, a closure, top level
    var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d);      x = p.div(d);   store64(&s, p.div(d)); # refused: bind both

A wrapper `fn w(..) { return p.div(d); }` forwards the pair, before or after its caller. `return g<T>(..)` /
`return p.m(..)` beside `return Ok(..)` is a whole variant (no mixed-return warning); the same spellings
returning ONE value still warn there.

FORWARD WRAPPERS: a caller parsed ABOVE its wrapper learns that the wrapper forwards a pair from a token
pass that runs before the wrapper is parsed (no locals exist yet). That pass finds `p` BY SCOPE (walking
back from the call; a block closed before it is skipped, and a closure looks through to the fn whose locals
it captures) and types it from a `: T` annotation (a param's `: *T` too), a struct literal, a
struct-returning call, or a generic's `: T` return bound at that call. A receiver typed only per instance (a
generic fn's own `p: T`) resolves when every struct's `_m` agrees on the pair flag; otherwise the call is
unknown — no flag, and no mixed-return warning. The first cut took the lexically LAST `p: T` and refused a
correct one-value bind; a wrong answer is worse than "unknown", so the pass returns "unknown" rather than
guess.

NOT COVERED (no call path types these receivers either): `a.b.m()`, `f().m()`, an untyped impl `self`. A
top-level `var v = f();` of any pair callee is not refused (the refusal is a fn-body check).

ECOSYSTEM: a new refusal in a consumer is a payload that was being dropped there. Fix it the way the message
says — `var tag, val = p.m(..);` — or propagate with `?`. Tests: tests/tcyr/crossos/method_pair_result.tcyr,
generic_struct_inference.tcyr (SQ rows); gates stack_enum_lossy_context.sh (axis 14),
stack_enum_mixed_return_warning.sh. See CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

[[entries]]
name = "typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616"
description = "A local `*i8` / `*i16` / `*i32` truncated the address it held (fixed 6.6.16); the per-site `p + n` step it documented was replaced by `sizeof(T)` everywhere at 6.6.17"
category = "cyrius"
---
⛔ BEFORE 6.6.16: `var buf: i64[4]; var p: *i8 = &buf;` — the `*T` ladder gave
*i8/*i16/*i32 pointer scale 1/2/4, PARSE_VAR wrote it as the local's SLTYPE,
and a positive SLTYPE below 8 is a narrow scalar's WIDTH. The slot stored
1/2/4 bytes of the address: `p == &buf` false, `load8(p + 1)` SIGSEGV, on
every backend (a Windows stack can sit below 4 GiB, which hid *i32 there).
Globals and params never had it (8-byte slots). Fix: `_sl_ptr_type` — a
`*T` local is SLTYPE 0 for those three (a plain 8-byte word), SLTYPE 8 for
every other *T, as before. Test: tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_pointer_slot_width.tcyr.
The 8-byte slot keeps its POINTER-ness for the expression type, so
`var q: *i16 = p;` / `= p + 1` are not "assigning non-pointer to typed
pointer". No emitted byte changed for the 8-byte case. (A `*T` PARAM, and a
`*T` local captured by a closure, still draw that warning when copied into a
typed pointer — pre-existing at 6.6.15.)

⟳ SUPERSEDED 6.6.17 — `p + n` now steps `sizeof(T)` at EVERY site (local, param,
capture, global in either zone, `*T` field, `: *T` result), `q - p` of two `*T` is the
element count, and a `*T` param / capture loads as a pointer (the warning above is gone).
The per-site table that stood here (local *i8/*i16/*i32 +1, other locals +8, params +1,
declaration-block globals +8, ...) is history: see language/features.cyml
`typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617`. `*p` still loads 8 bytes.
